  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point toward atomistic tensile studies of FCC high-entropy alloys across temperature and rate conditions. Most importantly, R7 studies MD deformation of the closely related CoNiCrFeMn nanowire and identifies partial-dislocation motion, FCC–HCP-related structural changes, stacking-fault-related behavior, and temperature-dependent deformation; R3 supplies bulk HEA MD tension at several temperatures and a high strain rate, while R1, R4, and R5 connect tensile deformation to partial dislocations and stacking faults. R2 and R12 provide temperature-dependent dislocation behavior in the closely related equiatomic CoCrFeNiMn alloy. However, the records do not specifically motivate a bulk single-crystal FeNiCrMn model, the particular crystallographic-direction and strain-rate sweep, vacancy-cluster-controlled dislocation nucleation, or the combined findings of rate-insensitive yield strength, increasing dislocation density with rate, and absence of twinning or bulk phase transformation. Thus they point in the right direction but leave substantial specific choices and mechanistic conclusions to a new study."
}
